# Object-Oriented Programming: Modeling the World in Objects

> Instead of a list of instructions, object-oriented programs are a
> society of objects that keep their own data and answer messages.

## 1. From Procedures to Objects

**Procedural** code is a recipe: data lives in variables, functions
transform them, and any function may touch any data — freedom that
becomes chaos as programs grow. **Object-oriented programming (OOP)**
flips the layout: bundle the data *with* the code allowed to touch
it. Each **object** guards its own state; the program becomes objects
sending each other messages.

## 2. Classes and Objects

A **class** is a blueprint; an **object** is an instance built from
it — one blueprint, many houses. A class declares **fields** (state)
and **methods** (behavior).

| Class `Car` | Kind | Example value for one object |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `make` | Field | "Maruti" |
| `speed` | Field | 42 |
| `accelerate()` | Method | adds 10 to `speed` |

@diagram tree id="vehicle-hierarchy" layout="tree" aria="Vehicle class hierarchy with Car and Motorbike subclasses, and ElectricCar extending Car"
  "Vehicle" -> "Car"
  "Vehicle" -> "Motorbike"
  "Car" -> "ElectricCar"
@/diagram

## 3. Encapsulation: Guard Your Own State

**Encapsulation** hides an object's fields behind its methods — mark
fields `private`, expose controlled accessors — to protect
**invariants**: rules that must always hold.

```python
class Account:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__balance = 0          # private by convention

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount <= 0: raise ValueError("must be positive")
        self.__balance += amount     # the ONLY way in
```

[important]
With the balance reachable only through `deposit`, no stray code can
ever set it to −500: the invariant cannot break at all.
[/important]

## 4. Inheritance: The Is-a Relationship

**Inheritance** lets a class adopt another's fields and methods —
`ElectricCar` **is a** `Vehicle` plus extras. Reuse is the reward;
fragility is the risk — parent changes ripple into every
descendant, so keep hierarchies shallow.

## 5. Polymorphism: One Call, Many Behaviors

**Polymorphism** ("many forms") lets the same call invoke different
code depending on the receiver's actual class. Iterating a list of
`Vehicle` objects calling `describe()`, each subclass supplies its
own **override**; the loop neither knows nor cares which.

[definition]
**Overriding** redefines an inherited method in a subclass;
**overloading** is a compile-time idea — same name, different
parameter lists, in one class.
[/definition]

## 6. Abstraction: Contract Before Mechanism

**Abstraction** exposes *what* an object does, hiding *how*. An
**interface** (or abstract class) states the contract — method names
and promises — and any class may implement it; dependents work with
every implementation, present or future.

| Tool | What it provides |
| --- | --- |
| Abstract class | Partial implementation + contract; single inheritance |
| Interface | Pure contract only; a class may implement many |

## 7. A Worked Example in JavaScript

```js
class Vehicle {
  constructor(make, speed) { this.make = make; this.speed = speed; }
  describe() { return this.make + " going " + this.speed + " km/h"; }
}
class ElectricCar extends Vehicle {
  constructor(make, battery) {
    super(make, 0);              // call parent constructor
    this.battery = battery;
  }
  describe() {                    // override
    return super.describe() + " | battery " + this.battery + "%";
  }
}

const garage = [new ElectricCar("Zap", 80), new Vehicle("Maruti", 60)];
garage.forEach(v => console.log(v.describe()));  // polymorphism at work
```

## 8. Composition vs Inheritance — and SOLID

Inheritance is powerful but brittle. Often the better tool is
**composition**: a `Car` *has an* `Engine` (a field) rather than *is
a* `MotorizedThing`. Favour "has-a" over "is-a" unless the hierarchy
is genuinely stable.

[tip]
SOLID, one line each: **S**ingle responsibility — one reason to
change; **O**pen/closed — extend, don't edit; **L**iskov — subclasses
usable wherever the parent is; **I**nterface segregation — many
small contracts; **D**ependency inversion — depend on abstractions.
[/tip]

## 9. Pitfalls — and OOP vs Functional

| Pitfall | Symptom | Cure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **God object** | One class "knows everything" | Split by responsibility |
| Deep hierarchy | Base change breaks ten children | Flatten; use composition |

OOP and **functional programming** are not enemies: OOP bundles
state with behavior; functional keeps data immutable and functions
pure. Modern code mixes both — state lives in objects, logic flows
through pure functions.

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Class vs object | Blueprint vs built instance |
| Encapsulation | Private fields + methods guard invariants |
| Polymorphism | Same call, subclass decides the behavior |
| Composition | Has-a beats is-a when in doubt |
